%S A402230 9,89,888,8879,88789,887888,8878878,88788777,887887769,8878877689,
%T A402230 88788776888,887887768879,8878877688789,88788776887888,
%U A402230 887887768878878,8878877688788777,88788776887887768,887887768878877679,8878877688788776789,88788776887887767888,887887768878877678878,8878877688788776788777
%N A402230 a(n) is the largest n-digit number from which no earlier term can be obtained by deleting digits one at a time, where a digit may be deleted only if it is the last digit or is not smaller than the digit currently following it.
%C A402230 Equivalently, a digit string u can be obtained from w if and only if the digits of u occur in w in order and every skipped digit before the first kept digit or between two kept digits is >= the next kept digit; skipped digits after the last kept digit are unrestricted. Strings are compared digit by digit, so 09 is not 9. For example, 889 is a subsequence of 8879 but cannot be obtained from it, since the 7 cannot be deleted while the larger 9 follows it. With unrestricted deletion the same rule gives A402229 (9, 88, 877, 8766, ...); the two sequences first differ at n = 2.
%C A402230 The sequence is finite: Schütte and Simpson (1985), extending Friedman's gap condition, proved that in every infinite sequence of words over a finite alphabet some word can be obtained from a later one by these deletions (a well-quasi-order). Yet the number N of terms satisfies N > F_(omega+8)(370693) (proved in the notes file), where F_0(x) = x+1, F_(k+1)(x) is F_k applied x+1 times starting at x, F_omega(x) = F_x(x), and F_(omega+k+1)(x) is F_(omega+k) applied x+1 times starting at x. For example F_1(x) = 2x+1 and F_omega(2) = 23; F_omega grows like Ackermann's function.
%C A402230 For n <= 1112083 the terms are determined by c = A320838 (see the Formula section), where c(0) = 0 and c(n) is the number of x < n with c(x) = c(n-1) and no larger value among c(x+1), ..., c(n-1): 1, 1, 2, 1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 1, 1, 2, .... The two comments of A320838, stated there without proof, become the copy law and the first appearances of the digits in the Formula section, and the notes file proves them (the first in a precise form). At n = 1112083 = A002104(10), c reaches 10 and a(n) ends in 0 for the first time.
%C A402230 The same rule applies to words over 0, 1, ..., b-1 with leading zeros allowed. For b = 2 it gives 1, 01, 000 and stops; base-2 numbers give only 1. For b = 3 the words begin 2, 12, 111, 1102, 11012, 110111, ...; exactly the 3rd to 87010th words begin with 11, the 87010th is 11 followed by 87008 zeros, and the next is 10 followed by 87009 twos. The notes file proves these facts and that the rule gives more than F_(omega+1)(87010) ternary words, more than F_(omega+1)(29002) ternary numbers, and for every b >= 3 more than F_(omega+b-2)(b-2) base-b numbers.
%C A402230 If each digit d is read as the priority 9-d, deleting a digit other than the last is a "superseding" step of the priority channel systems of Haase, Schmitz and Schnoebelen (2014); their length bound, based on Schmitz and Schnoebelen (2011), gives (by an argument in the notes file) N < H_q^(omega_21)(2) for an unspecified polynomial q, in the notation of the Formula section.
%C A402230 For each fixed number b of symbols, Peano arithmetic proves that the rule stops. Schütte and Simpson proved that Peano arithmetic cannot prove the well-quasi-order property for all b at once, not even in a finite form with linearly bounded word lengths (their Theorems II and III, stated for the weaker gap condition without the restriction before the first kept digit, which carries over to this order). Whether Peano arithmetic proves that this rule stops for every b is open. See the notes file in the Links section for proofs, sources and further data.
