%H A402230 Christoph Haase, Sylvain Schmitz and Philippe Schnoebelen, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1301.5500">The power of priority channel systems</a>, arXiv:1301.5500 [cs.LO], 2013-2014; Logical Methods in Computer Science 10(4:4) (2014).
%H A402230 Graham Higman, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1112/plms/s3-2.1.326">Ordering by divisibility in abstract algebras</a>, Proc. London Math. Soc. (3) 2 (1952), 326-336.
%H A402230 Michael Rathjen, Jeroen Van der Meeren and Andreas Weiermann, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1505.01359">Ordinal notation systems corresponding to Friedman's linearized well-partial-orders with gap-condition</a>, arXiv:1505.01359 [math.LO], 2015; Arch. Math. Logic 56 (2017), 607-638.
%H A402230 Sylvain Schmitz and Philippe Schnoebelen, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1103.4399v2">Multiply-Recursive Upper Bounds with Higman's Lemma</a>, arXiv:1103.4399 [cs.LO], 2011; extended version of the paper in ICALP 2011, Lecture Notes in Comput. Sci. 6756, 441-452; see Proposition 5.2.
%H A402230 Kurt Schütte and Stephen G. Simpson, <a href="https://eudml.org/doc/138039">Ein in der reinen Zahlentheorie unbeweisbarer Satz über endliche Folgen von natürlichen Zahlen</a>, Arch. Math. Logik Grundlag. 25 (1985), 75-89.
%H A402230 Stephen G. Simpson, <a href="https://sgslogic.net/t20/papers/nichtbeweisbarkeit-aml-1985.pdf">Nichtbeweisbarkeit von gewissen kombinatorischen Eigenschaften endlicher Bäume</a>, Arch. Math. Logik Grundlag. 25 (1985), 45-65; presents unpublished results of Harvey Friedman.
%F A402230 a(n) = 10*a(n-1) - A320838(n) for 2 <= n <= 1112083; in words, a(n) is a(n-1) with its last digit lowered by 1, followed by the digit 10 - A320838(n). Equivalently, a(n) = 10^n - Sum_{k=1..n} A320838(k)*10^(n-k) for n <= 1112083.
%F A402230 For m = A002104(j) with 1 <= j <= 9; that is, m = 1, 3, 8, 24, 89, 415, 2372, 16072, 125673: a(m+i) = (a(m)-1)*10^i + a(i) for 1 <= i <= m, but not for i = m+1; in words, a(m+i) is a(m)-1 followed by the digits of a(i).
%F A402230 For 0 <= k <= 9, the digit 9-k first appears in a(A002104(k)+1); that is, in a(1), a(2), a(4), a(9), a(25), a(90), a(416), a(2373), a(16073), a(125674); so for 0 <= k <= 8 the first appearances of 9-k and 8-k are A000522(k) terms apart.
%F A402230 Let N be the number of terms. Then, N < H_h^(omega_21)(h(3092)) where h(x) = 2^(2^((x+10)^2)) + 1, omega_21 is a tower of 21 omegas, and H_h is the Hardy hierarchy with step h: H_h^0(x) = x, H_h^(alpha+1)(x) = H_h^alpha(h(x)), and H_h^lambda(x) = H_h^(lambda[x])(x) for limit ordinals lambda (definitions and the bound for b symbols in the notes file); a(N) = 10^(N-1).
%e A402230 a(1) = 9. a(2) = 89: each of 90, ..., 99 begins with 9, and deleting its last digit gives 9. In 89 the 8 cannot be deleted, because the 9 after it is larger, and deleting the 9 leaves 8; so 9 cannot be obtained from 89.
%e A402230 a(3) = 888: a 3-digit number greater than 888 begins with 9, begins with 89, or is 889. In the first two cases, deleting final digits gives 9 or 89; from 889, deleting the first 8 gives 89 (allowed, since the next digit is also 8). From 888 only 88 and 8 can be obtained.
%e A402230 a(4) = 8879: a 4-digit number greater than 8879 begins with 9, 89, 888 or 889, and in each case an earlier term can be obtained from it (from 889x delete x and then the first 8). From 8879 one deletion gives 879 or 887, and further deletions give 79, 87, 88, 7 and 8; none of these is 9, 89 or 888.
%e A402230 Over the symbols 0 and 1 the rule gives 1, 01, 000 and stops: 1 can be obtained from every word that begins with 1, and 01 from every word in which some 0 is directly followed by 1; the remaining words consist of zeros, and 000 can be obtained from each of them that has 3 or more digits.
%e A402230 The terms a(9), ..., a(16) are 88788776 = a(8) - 1 followed by the digits of a(1), ..., a(8):
%e A402230    n  a(n)              digits after 88788776
%e A402230    9  887887769         9
%e A402230   10  8878877689        89
%e A402230   11  88788776888       888
%e A402230   12  887887768879      8879
%e A402230   13  8878877688789     88789
%e A402230   14  88788776887888    887888
%e A402230   15  887887768878878   8878878
%e A402230   16  8878877688788777  88788777
%e A402230 Then a(17) = 88788776887887768, whereas 88788776 followed by a(9) would be 88788776887887769.
%o A402230 (Python)
%o A402230 # Residual suffixes retain every possible strong gap embedding.
%o A402230 # A prefix containing an earlier term forbids all its extensions.
%o A402230 def search(p, n, residuals):
%o A402230     if "" in residuals:
%o A402230         return None
%o A402230     if len(p) == n:
%o A402230         return p
%o A402230     for d in ("9876543210" if p else "987654321"):
%o A402230         r = {u for u in residuals if u[0] <= d}
%o A402230         r.update(u[1:] for u in residuals if u[0] == d)
%o A402230         w = search(p + d, n, r)
%o A402230         if w is not None:
%o A402230             return w
%o A402230 terms = []
%o A402230 for n in range(1, 26):
%o A402230     w = search("", n, set(terms))
%o A402230     if w is None:
%o A402230         break
%o A402230     terms.append(w)
%o A402230     print(w)
